.TH CTI-AGENT 1 "2026-10-12" "cti-agent 1.3" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
cti-agent \- run a fleet command by hand as the service account; and the daily mailbox ingest binary
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo cti\-agent
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent
.IB lane .py
.RI [ args ...]
.PP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/agent/cti\-agent
.RB [ \-\-lookback
.IR DURATION ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Two programs share this name.
.SS The operator wrapper
.I /usr/local/bin/cti\-agent
is a short bash script written by
.B install\-fedora.sh
(root-owned, mode 0755). It is the single supported way to run a fleet
command by hand on the Fedora layout, with the same paths the systemd units
set. Run it with
.BR sudo :
it hops to the service account itself, which needs no password only when the
caller is already root.
.PP
In order, it:
.IP 1. 4
Sets
.B set \-euo pipefail
and exports exactly five variables:
.RS
.EX
FLEET_HOME=/var/lib/cti\-agent
FLEET_CODE=/opt/cti\-agent
FLEET_ENV=/etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env
FLEET_FEEDS=/etc/cti\-agent/feeds.txt
HOME=/var/lib/cti\-agent
.EE
.RE
.IP 2. 4
Changes to
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent ,
as the units do with
.BR WorkingDirectory= ,
so a relative path in
.I fleet.env
resolves to the same file for a hand run and a timer run.
.IP 3. 4
Takes the first argument as the command name (exiting with a usage message
if there is none) and passes the rest through unchanged.
.IP 4. 4
Replaces itself
.RB ( exec )
with the command described under COMMANDS, run through
.RS
.EX
sudo \-u ctiagent \e
  \-\-preserve\-env=FLEET_HOME,FLEET_CODE,FLEET_ENV,FLEET_FEEDS,HOME \e
  /bin/bash \-c '<load fleet.env, restore the five, exec>'
.EE
.RE
.IP 5. 4
As
.BR ctiagent ,
before the command starts, loads
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env
with
.B set \-a
as the runners do, then puts the five variables back to the values above, so
a stale
.B FLEET_HOME
in
.I fleet.env
cannot point a hand run at different files.
.PP
Only those five variables cross the
.B sudo
boundary; everything else in the caller's environment is reset by sudo, and
then
.I fleet.env
is loaded.
.B GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS=168 sudo cti\-agent ...
therefore runs with the configured value, not 168. Use a command's own flag
(for example
.BR "run\-digest \-\-lookback" )
for one-off overrides.
.PP
Since 1.3 the wrapper loads
.I fleet.env
for every command (step 5), so a hand run sees the same settings as the
timer, including for the commands that do not load it themselves
.RB ( cti\-kev ,
.BR enrich.py ,
.BR brief.py ,
.BR scout.py ,
.BR fleet\-db ).
Before 1.3 those ran on their built-in defaults by hand.
.PP
The wrapper does not check the name against a list. The usage message names
the common commands, but any file in
.I /opt/cti\-agent/bin
or
.I /opt/cti\-agent/lanes
can be run. It is a convenience, not a security boundary: the caller is
already root.
.SS The ingest binary
.I /opt/cti\-agent/agent/cti\-agent
is built from
.B cmd/cti\-agent
in the source clone at
.B $CTI_AGENT_DIR
.RI ( /opt/cti\-agent/agent ).
Unlike every other Go command it is
.B not
installed in
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/bin ,
so
.B sudo cti\-agent cti\-agent
fails with
.IR "command not found" .
It is run by
.BR run\-digest (1)
step 1 for the daily digest (the weekly does not read the mailbox), from
.B $CTI_AGENT_DIR
as the working directory, with
.B REPORT_PATH
set to
.IR $FLEET_HOME/reports/raw\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.md .
If the compiled binary is missing,
.B run\-digest
falls back to
.BR "go run ./cmd/cti\-agent" .
.PP
One run:
.TP
.B Reads the mailbox
Every message in
.B GRAPH_FOLDER
of
.B GRAPH_MAILBOX
received in the last
.B GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS
(or
.BR \-\-lookback ),
through Microsoft Graph.
.TP
.B Skips its own mail
A message carrying a correctly signed
.B X\-CTI\-Agent\-Sent
header (an unsigned or forged one is ignored), or sent from
.B GRAPH_MAILBOX
itself, is the fleet's own report coming back and is dropped before
anything else sees it. The count of skipped messages is logged.
.TP
.B Extracts CVEs
CVE identifiers in each subject and body, deduplicated and sorted.
.TP
.B Holds back Patch Tuesday CVEs
CVEs named in this month's or last month's Patch Tuesday manifest
.RI ( $FLEET_HOME/state/patchtuesday\-YYYY\-MM.json )
are left out of the table, but only when that manifest is marked
.IR sent .
No manifest, an unsent one, or an unreadable one (logged as a WARNING)
means every CVE is reported.
.TP
.B Looks each CVE up
With
.BR LOOKUP_PROVIDER :
.B qualys
asks the KnowledgeBase and Host Detection APIs whether the CVE is present;
.BR none ,
.B noop
or empty reports every CVE as UNKNOWN;
.B crowdstrike
is a stub that also reports UNKNOWN. A lookup that fails becomes an UNKNOWN
row with the reason, not a failed run.
.TP
.B Writes the report
Markdown, to
.BR REPORT_PATH ,
mode 0600. Its header gives the mailbox, the window, the message counts and
the held CVEs, and says
.B CATCH-UP RUN
when
.B \-\-lookback
was used. Hostnames appear as
.B REPORT_HOSTNAMES
says.
.TP
.B Records what it read
After the report is written, and only then, every message it read is added
to the processed-message log, which is
.BR cti\-mailbox (1)'s
only authority to move mail. A failure to write the log is a WARNING, not a
failure; with neither
.B FLEET_PROCESSED_LOG
nor
.B FLEET_HOME
set, nothing is recorded and a NOTE says cleanup will move nothing.
.PP
It prints one line on standard output:
.IR "No CVEs found. Wrote PATH" ,
.IR "All N CVE(s) found were held for the monthly Patch Tuesday synopsis..." ,
or
.IR "Wrote PATH" .
Progress goes to standard error.
.PP
The ingest binary does not send mail and does not move, flag or delete any
message.
.SH COMMANDS
The wrapper's command forms. Arguments after the command are passed through
untouched.
.TP
.IB name .py " \fR[\fIargs\fR...]"
A name ending in
.B .py
runs
.B /usr/bin/python3
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/lanes/name .py
as
.BR ctiagent .
Installed lanes:
.BR enrich.py ,
.BR scout.py ,
.BR brief.py ,
.BR mailer.py .
.TP
.IR name " [" args ...]
Any other name runs
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/bin/name
as
.BR ctiagent .
That directory holds the runners
.RB ( run\-digest ,
.BR run\-checkin ,
.BR run\-patchtuesday ,
.BR run\-appscan ,
.BR run\-domains ,
.BR run\-mailbox\-cleanup ),
the board and database tools
.RB ( fleet\-db ,
.BR fleet\-board ),
and the Go commands
.RB ( cti\-alert ,
.BR cti\-budget ,
.BR cti\-kev ,
.BR cti\-patchtuesday ,
.BR cti\-mailbox ,
.BR cti\-mailer ,
.BR cti\-jira ,
.BR cti\-appscan ,
.BR cti\-appreport ,
.BR cti\-domains ).
.SH OPTIONS
These are the ingest binary's. The wrapper has none of its own; anything
after the command name belongs to that command.
.PP
Go's flag syntax: the option may also be written with a single dash
.RB ( \-lookback ),
and the value may follow
.B =
or a space.
.TP
.BI \-\-lookback " DURATION"
Read this far back for this run only, instead of
.BR GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS ,
as a Go duration
.RB ( 168h ,
.BR 72h ).
For the morning after the lane was off. The run logs a
.B CATCH-UP RUN
line and the report header says so; findings an earlier digest reported
appear again. A positive value longer than 90 days
.RB ( 2160h )
is refused as a probable typo. Zero, or any negative value, is treated as
not given and the configured window is used without comment.
Normally passed through
.BR "run\-digest \-\-lookback" .
Default:\~\fB0\fR (use \fBGRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS\fR).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The wrapper sets
.BR FLEET_HOME ,
.BR FLEET_CODE ,
.BR FLEET_ENV ,
.B FLEET_FEEDS
and
.B HOME
as described above, and reads nothing else.
.PP
The ingest binary loads
.B FLEET_ENV
(else
.IR $FLEET_HOME/fleet.env ,
else
.IR /var/lib/cti\-agent/fleet.env )
into its environment at start; variables already set win, so under
.B run\-digest
(which has sourced the file) this changes nothing. It then reads:
.TP
.BR TENANT_ID ", " CLIENT_ID ", " CLIENT_SECRET
The Entra app registration. Required.
.I fleet.env
section 1.
.TP
.B GRAPH_MAILBOX
The mailbox read. Required, no default. Section 1.
.TP
.B GRAPH_FOLDER
Folder within it, by display name. Default:\~\fBinbox\fR. Section 1.
.TP
.B GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS
The ingest window, a positive integer number of hours.
Default:\~\fB24\fR. Section 1.
.TP
.B LOOKUP_PROVIDER
.BR qualys ,
.BR none ,
.B noop
or
.BR crowdstrike ;
anything else is an error.
Default:\~\fBqualys\fR. Section 2.
.TP
.BR QUALYS_BASE_URL ", " QUALYS_USERNAME ", " QUALYS_PASSWORD
The Qualys API pod and a read-only account. Required when
.B LOOKUP_PROVIDER
is
.BR qualys .
Section 2.
.TP
.B QUALYS_KB_CACHE
The CVE-to-QID cache, 0600. A relative path resolves against the working
directory, which under
.B run\-digest
is
.BR $CTI_AGENT_DIR ;
keep it absolute.
Default:\~\fI./qualys_kb_cache.json\fR. Section 3.
.TP
.B QUALYS_KB_MAX_AGE_HOURS
How long the cache is trusted before it is refreshed, a positive integer.
Default:\~\fB168\fR. Section 3.
.TP
.B REPORT_PATH
Where the Markdown report goes.
.B run\-digest
sets it per day.
Default:\~\fI./cti\-agent\-report.md\fR. Section 3.
.TP
.B REPORT_HOSTNAMES
.B full
(real hostnames),
.B redact
(salted pseudonyms
.BR host\-xxxxxxxx )
or
.B count
(counts only). Anything else means
.BR full .
Default:\~\fBfull\fR. Section 5.
.TP
.B REPORT_REDACTION_SALT
Salt for
.B redact
mode. Empty makes the pseudonyms guessable. Section 5.
.TP
.BR MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS ", " MAILBOX_MIN_AGE_HOURS
The cleanup lane's window and grace (defaults
.B 72
and
.BR 48 ).
Not used by the ingest, but validated by it: the run refuses to start if
.B MAILBOX_MIN_AGE_HOURS
is non-zero and less than
.BR GRAPH_LOOKBACK_HOURS ,
or is not less than
.BR MAILBOX_LOOKBACK_HOURS .
Section 10.
.TP
.B FLEET_PROCESSED_LOG
Where the processed-message log goes.
Default:\~\fI$FLEET_HOME/state/processed\-messages.json\fR. Section 4, mailbox cleanup.
.TP
.B FLEET_HOME
Locates the Patch Tuesday manifests and the default processed-message log.
Section 3.
.TP
.B CTI_AGENT_DIR
Read by
.BR run\-digest ,
not by the binary: the directory holding the source clone and the compiled
.BR cti\-agent .
Section 3.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/cti\-agent
The wrapper, rewritten on every install.
.TP
.I /opt/cti\-agent/agent/cti\-agent
The ingest binary, rebuilt on every install from the clone in the same
directory.
.TP
.IR /opt/cti\-agent/bin/ ", " /opt/cti\-agent/lanes/
What the wrapper can run; root:root 0755, read-only to the service.
.TP
.I /etc/cti\-agent/fleet.env
Settings and credentials;
.B root:ctiagent
0640.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/
The service account's home and working directory, 0750.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/reports/raw\-YYYY\-MM\-DD.md
The ingest report for that day, 0600. It can name real hosts.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/processed\-messages.json
Messages the ingest read, kept 14 days, 0600.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/patchtuesday\-YYYY\-MM.json
Patch Tuesday manifests, read to decide what to hold back.
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/qualys_kb_cache.json
The KnowledgeBase cache, 0600.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The wrapper exits with the status of the command it ran. It exits
.B 1
itself when no command is given or
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent
cannot be entered (run without
.BR sudo );
.B sudo
exits 1 when the named command does not exist.
.PP
The ingest binary:
.TP
.B 0
The report was written. Individual lookups that failed are UNKNOWN rows in
it; an unreadable manifest or an unwritten processed-message log is a
WARNING, not a failure.
.TP
.B 1
No report: configuration missing or invalid (the message names the
variables); an out-of-range
.BR \-\-lookback ;
the Graph read failed; an unsupported
.BR LOOKUP_PROVIDER ;
or the report could not be written.
.TP
.B 2
An unknown option or an unparseable duration.
.B \-\-help
prints the usage and exits 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
Rehearse today's digest. It sends nothing, but the ingest still runs: the
raw report is written and the messages read are recorded as processed:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-digest daily \-\-dry\-run
.EE
.PP
Catch up after the timer was off for a week:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent run\-digest daily \-\-lookback 168h
.EE
.PP
Other commands through the wrapper:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-db recent
sudo cti\-agent cti\-budget status
sudo cti\-agent mailer.py \-\-check
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-digest (1),
.BR cti\-mailbox (1),
.BR cti\-patchtuesday (1),
.BR cti\-budget (1),
.BR cti\-alert (1),
.BR sudo (8)
.PP
RUNBOOK.md sections 5, 8 and 11a; fleet\-kit/README.md, "Command reference".
